Durarara!! AU
Chapter 27 – Part 2 
Mika was already gone.
In sleep she shifted closer, threw a leg over his hip, and settled there like the suite had been built around that exact geometry. Warm. Heavy. Unaware.
Curtis stared at the inside of his own elbow.
“Okay. I’m feeling the pressure now.”
He tried to ease her leg back to her side of the mattress.
Mika made a small sound that was not a word and clenched on — arm across his chest, knee hooked, the full stubborn weight of a girl who had decided, even unconscious, that she was not going back in a drawer.
Curtis froze.
Oh dear.
Not Celty’s nightwear. Not a choice she was making with her eyes open. Just a reconstructed body hunting warmth and winning.
He stopped trying to move her.
Waking her to say your leg felt cruel. Letting the far bed win after he had told her he preferred her not scared felt worse.
He exhaled through his teeth, long black braids caught under her shoulder, and stared at the ceiling like it might offer a third option.
Morning. Address. Mikado. Pink rose. Do not panic because a sleeping person treated you like furniture that doesn’t leave.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima slept like she meant to stay.
Curtis Nightstar lay trapped under kindness
and called it pressure
because I miss you, Celty was already occupying the other half of the bed.
Curtis turned his head as far as Mika’s arm would allow.
Ikebukuro sat beyond the glass — signs, trains, a sky that never went fully dark. The hunt was out there somewhere. Shizuo. Celty. Seiji. A family that wanted this girl back in a tank.
He looked at the window anyway.
“The night’s beautiful.”
Mika did not answer. She was a warm weight across his chest and hip, breath even, pink rose a small dark shape on the nightstand.
He said it to the glass. To the city that kept handing him people. To a brother who was not on the other bed. To a Dullahan who did not know which window he was behind.
Beautiful did not mean safe.
It just meant, for this hour, the suite was high enough that the ugly parts of the street looked like light.
Curtis let his eyes close on the view.
On the fifth floor, a wanderer complimented the dark
while a missing girl slept on him like the night had finally chosen a side.
Mika inched closer.
Not a choice. Sleep doing the math. Her forehead found the side of his jaw. The arm across his chest tightened. The leg over his hip claimed another inch of hotel white until there was no strip left to argue with.
Curtis’s thoughts went flat and loud.
Okay. I’m losing it.
Mika. Wake up and look at what you’re doing.
He did not say it. Waking her to scold an unconscious cling felt like punishing her for feeling safe. He stared at the beautiful night like it might take attendance.
Elsewhere, unheard, a boy who had always been worse at leaving people alone snorted in a place that was not a room.
Stop being a pussy.
Curtis, who could not hear him, still flinched like the sentence had landed.
He kept his hands where they were — one under his head, one dead-still at his side — and breathed like the suite was a forest and sudden movement would startle something rare.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima migrated in her sleep toward the only warm thing that had not tried to own her.
Justice laughed in a register the living were not allowed to have.
And Curtis Nightstar lost another inch of mattress
without losing the rule:
if she was not scared, he could survive being uncomfortable.
An hour passed.
Curtis drifted off the way he always did — late, shallow, arm still thrown over his own eyes like he could hold the world off even unconscious. Long black braids spilled across the pillow. The night stayed beautiful in the glass and did not knock.
Mika woke first.
For a second she did not know which life she was in. Lab. Club. Hotel. Then the details arrived in order: pink rose on the nightstand. His heartbeat under her palm. Her own leg hooked over his hip. Her forehead against his jaw. No strip of white left between them.
She froze.
Oh.
She had told him not to make it weird. Then she had done the whole weird herself, asleep, like her body had voted without her.
Curtis did not stir. Whatever he dreamed, it was not this. His face under the braids looked younger when he was not performing I’m fine.
Mika’s first instinct was to yank back and pretend the far bed had won.
Her second instinct, quieter, was the same one that had sat down on his mattress: scared is how I walked into that building.
She eased her leg back a few inches. Left her arm where it was. Heat crawled up her reconstructed cheeks anyway.
“…You’re lucky you’re asleep,” she whispered at his collarbone. “I’d never live this down.”
She did not wake him.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima catalogued the damage —
too close, too warm, too honest for a girl who had borrowed a face —
and stayed in the wreckage on purpose,
because pulling all the way off felt more like going back in the drawer
than like dignity.
Mika watched him sleep.
The performance was gone. No chuckle. No I’m fine. Just a tall boy with his arm over his face and braids spilled like he had fallen out of a forest into a hotel.
A single tear cut the edge of his cheek and vanished into the pillow.
He whispered it like a door he still tried at night.
“Justice…”
Mika’s breath caught.
She knew the name now the way you knew a ghost from a story — brother from the woods, dead, the reason Japan had been a final stop. She did not know the face. She knew the sound Curtis made when the name got out without permission.
She did not shake him.
Her thumb hovered, then carefully wiped the track before it could reach his mouth. The skin under it was warm. Human. Not a rumor.
“…He’s not here,” she whispered, barely air. “I am. That’s not the same. I know.”
Curtis did not wake. The next breath came rougher, then evened.
Mika left her arm across his chest.
On the fifth floor, a missing girl kept watch over a missing-poster boy
while he asked a dead brother to come back
in a voice small enough to fit in a suite
and too large for Ikebukuro to answer.
Curtis’s eyes shot open.
He sat up so fast Mika had to catch herself on an elbow or go over the far side of the mattress.
“Wha—? Where am I.”
The suite resolved in pieces. High window. Pink rose. Blue blazer on the chair. A girl with someone else’s borrowed beauty staring at him like she had been caught stealing a secret.
“Oh…”
Long black braids hung in his face. He dragged a hand across his cheek and found it damp.
Mika stayed half-propped, voice small on purpose.
“Hotel. Fifth floor. You’re safe. You fell asleep. I did too.” A beat. “You said a name. I didn’t wake you for it.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked to the wet on his fingers, then away.
“…Yeah. Sorry.” He sat with his elbows on his knees, breathing like he had come back from a longer distance than a nap. “Forest. Fire. He was nine. I was ten. Then I was here.”
He glanced at how little space she had left him.
“You okay? I didn’t—” He stopped. Started again. “I didn’t mean to make you watch that.”
Mika shook her head.
“You didn’t make me. I was already awake.” She nodded at the nightstand. “Rose is still pink. City hasn’t knocked. You can lie back down if the ceiling isn’t hunting you.”
Curtis let out a long breath.
“Oh,” he said again, quieter, like the room had finally finished introducing itself.
On the fifth floor, the dream let him go.
Mika Harima did not ask who Justice had been in the fire.
She only stayed in arm’s reach
while Curtis Nightstar remembered which city he had woken up in.
Curtis sat up the rest of the way and looked out the window.
Ikebukuro’s night sat there, still beautiful, and did not help.
The color went out of his eyes the way it did when the present lost the argument — pale, far, like he was watching the glass from a forest that did not have a fifth floor.
Mika saw it happen.
“Curtis.”
No answer. His breathing stayed even. His body was in the suite. The rest of him had stepped sideways into whatever Justice had opened.
She shifted onto her knees on the mattress, not grabbing yet.
“Hey. Nightstar. Look at me.” Still nothing. She snapped her fingers once, sharp, hotel-quiet. “You’re not in the woods. You’re not ten. You’re in a room you paid for with a black card and a girl you pulled out of a club.”
The pale held.
Mika put two fingers against his jaw and turned his face away from the glass. Her voice dropped.
“Come back. I’m not doing the drawer thing alone while you go missing behind your own eyes.”
A blink.
Color leaked in at the edges — gold and whatever the other one was — slow, like a tide that had to remember the shore.
Curtis’s mouth parted.
“…Sorry.” The word came from a long way off. “Window does that sometimes. Too much sky. Looks like canopy if I don’t fight it.”
Mika did not let go of his jaw until the pale was gone.
“Then don’t stare at it like it’s a door.” She exhaled. “You scared me. Stay in the room this time.”
On the fifth floor, the night stayed on the other side of the glass.
Curtis Nightstar came back into his face.
And Mika Harima learned there was a version of him that could leave without standing up.
Curtis sighed, the pale gone but the night still sitting in his shoulders.
“Sorry. But I can’t sleep. Or rather… I don’t want to.”
He swung his legs off the bed like the mattress had become another window. Crossed the suite. Took the bar stool and folded forward until his head was in his hands, long black braids hanging between his elbows, a silhouette against the city.
The pink rose stayed on the nightstand.
Mika watched him from the bed for three breaths.
Then she followed, barefoot, and took the stool beside him without asking if the bar was invitation-only. She did not touch him this time. She set her chin in her palm and faced the same dark bottles he was not drinking.
“…Dreams or the window.” Not a question that needed an answer. “You don’t have to go back to the mattress. I’m not going back to the far bed either.”
A smaller beat.
“If you want quiet, I’ll be quiet. If you want the small guitar again, say so. If you want me to talk about stupid things so your head stays in Ikebukuro, I can do that too.”
Curtis’s voice came out muffled against his palms.
“Quiet’s fine.”
Mika nodded at the bottles.
“Quiet it is.”
On the fifth floor, insomnia took the bar.
A missing girl kept the next stool.
And Curtis Nightstar rested his head in his hands
because sleep had started asking for a brother
he could not bring into the room.
Curtis kept his head in his hands, voice aimed at the bar top.
“What’s Seiji like? I only saw him briefly on the first day.”
Mika was quiet long enough that the mini-fridge hummed.
“…That’s a loaded question to ask a girl who isn’t supposed to exist.”
She turned her glass — empty — like it might help.
“Seiji Yagiri doesn’t do brief. You saw the version that showed up late, told the class he wasn’t coming back, and left. That’s him. School was in the way. Friends were in the way. His sister, the company, the whole stupid family — in the way.” A thin breath. “I was the only thing that wasn’t.”
She stared at the dark bottles.
“He’s not cruel the way nameless men in alleys are cruel. He’s worse in a different direction. He looks at you like you’re the only real object in the city and everything else is furniture. When I was just Mika, that felt like love. After the tank—” her fingers flexed “—it feels like a lock.”
A smaller, crooked beat.
“He would burn Ikebukuro down to keep a face he recognizes. He already let the rest of his life die for it. That’s what he’s like.”
She glanced at Curtis’s braids.
“You asked like he’s a classmate. He’s not. He’s the reason I was in that club instead of a desk. If you meet him again, don’t do brief. He doesn’t hear it.”
On the fifth floor, Seiji Yagiri became a person instead of a first-day rumor.
Curtis Nightstar listened with his head in his hands.
And Mika Harima told the truth without dressing it up as a love story.
Curtis lifted his head from his hands just enough to look at her.
“I’m sorry you gave up so much. That’s not fair to anyone. Love shouldn’t be a one-way street.”
Mika went still on the next stool.
“I hope in the end you obtain what you want. Because all this you’re going through — you don’t deserve it. I don’t care what the rumors say. You’re an amazing person, Mika.” A beat, quieter. “I hope Seiji sees that one day. Not the face that went through reconstruction. Not Celty. But you. As Mika.”
The suite fridge hummed like it was trying to fill the space he had just opened.
Mika’s mouth trembled once, then flattened.
“…You can’t just say that at a hotel bar.” Her voice had gone thin. “People use my name like it’s a product. Seiji uses it like a vow. The company used it like clay. You don’t get to sit there with pale-eye problems and tell me I’m Mika like that’s simple.”
She wiped at her face with the heel of her hand, angry at the wet.
“Thank you.” It came out small after the snap. “I mean that. Nobody’s hoped that for me out loud since before the tank. They hope I look right. They hope I go home. They hope Seiji stops ruining his life.” A shaky breath. “You’re the first person tonight who hoped I get something.”
She stared at the bottles.
“If he ever sees me — the me that sat on your bed and stole your chest and watched you cry for a dead brother — I don’t know if he’d still want that. That’s the part that scares me. Not the hunt.”
A smaller, crooked smile.
“You’re still an idiot for being kind to a rumor. Don’t stop yet.”
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima was addressed by her own name
and not by a stolen head.
Curtis Nightstar had called her amazing
without asking her to be anyone’s ending.
Curtis kept his elbows on the bar.
“Hey Mika. Can I ask you something.”
“Sure.”
“Who did your surgery? There’s not many doctors with that skill set.”
Mika’s fingers stopped turning the empty glass.
“…You would ask the detective question.”
She looked at the bottles, not at him.
“Yagiri Pharmaceuticals arranged it. Seiji’s sister. Namie. She woke me up on a table after he—” A small pause. “After he thought he’d finished me. She offered a deal. Make my face match the thing he actually loved. Then drugs, so I wouldn’t remember I used to be anyone else.”
Her mouth twisted.
“I took the face. I ran before the drugs.”
Another beat. This one had a name in it.
“The one who cut was a young underground doctor. White coat. Talked like the whole thing was interesting instead of evil. He already knew what to call the head. He told me to say Celty if I wanted Seiji to look at me.” She glanced at Curtis. “Shinra. That’s what they called him.”
The suite went very quiet.
Mika watched his face the way she had watched his eyes go pale at the window.
“You know that name,” she said. Not a question. “Don’t lie and tell me you don’t. You just made a face like the city got smaller.”
On the fifth floor, the dinner at Russia Sushi found the girl on the next stool.
Curtis Nightstar had asked a skill-set question.
Mika Harima had answered with the doctor who lived with the woman he missed —
and the hunt was no longer only in the street. It was already at a table he had paid for.
Curtis propped himself up on the stool like the bar had just become a crime scene.
“Fuck all kinds of duck.”
Mika blinked. “What?”
“It’s a saying. When shit hits the fan.”
“No, I get it.” Her eyes narrowed. “What’s the problem.”
Curtis dragged a hand down his face.
“A separate one I got roped into.” Long black braids shifted. “Was there any other doctor with him.”
Mika went still, searching.
The table. The lights. Namie’s voice making a deal. Shinra’s too-bright interest. Then the slide toward dark — drugs or blood loss or both — and under it, before the world shut off:
A woman’s voice.
Not Namie. Younger. Clinical in the same ugly way, but warmer around the edges, like she already knew the man in the white coat and was talking over a body instead of to one. A laugh that did not belong in an operating room. Something about hold still or he always gets sloppy when he’s excited. She had not seen the face. Only the sound.
“…There was a woman,” Mika said slowly. “Before I passed out. I didn’t see her. I just heard her. She talked like she belonged next to him. Not a nurse. Not Namie.” A thin swallow. “Why. Do you know her too.”
Curtis’s jaw flexed.
On the fifth floor, Russia Sushi grew teeth.
Shinra had the knife.
Mei had the laugh in the dark.
And Mika Harima had just handed Curtis Nightstar a second doctor
he had already sat across from
without knowing the table was built on her face.
Curtis looked at her like the empty glass had just become evidence that mattered.
“I’m going to give you the world for what you just gave me. Thank you, Mika.”
Mika stared.
“…That’s a lot of sentence for a voice I heard while I was passing out.”
She pulled her knees up onto the rungs of the stool, suddenly smaller.
“Don’t thank me like I handed you a gift. I handed you a nightmare I was in. If that name — if those names — are people you eat dinner with, I need you to not get stupid and walk back into their kitchen wearing my story on your face.”
A beat. Softer, against her will.
“…You’re welcome. I guess. Nobody’s ever said they’d give me the world for telling the truth. They usually want me quieter after.”
She flicked a look at the pink rose on the nightstand.
“World can wait. Morning address first. Then you can be dramatic.”
On the fifth floor, Curtis Nightstar paid for information the only way he knew how —
too big, too earnest, too much —
and Mika Harima, who had been clay and a rumor,
did not know what to do with being valuable on purpose.
Curtis stood.
He took Mika’s hand before she could ask why, spun her off the bar stool in one easy turn — 6’3” making the suite look like it had been built for the move — and dipped her like the fifth floor had suddenly become a dance floor.
Mika made a sound that was half yelp, half laugh. The pink-rose nightstand watched. Her reconstructed hair fell back. His smirk arrived late, like it had to check that she wasn’t scared first.
“Sorry. I know you’ve been through so much. But somehow we met, and you helped me with a separate problem I’m facing.” He held the dip a second longer than manners required. “Mika. I’m very grateful for meeting you.”
He brought her back up.
Mika’s hands were on his shoulders whether she had planned it or not. Color sat high on borrowed cheekbones.
“…You’re insane.” Not a rejection. A diagnosis. “People don’t dip missing girls at two in the morning after asking who cut their face.”
She didn’t shove him off immediately.
“Grateful is fine. World-giving is fine. Twirling me like the hunt isn’t downstairs is—” Her mouth fought a smile and lost. “Put me down properly before I get used to it. Seiji never danced. He just stared.”
A smaller breath.
“You’re welcome. For the voice. For the name. Don’t spend the gratitude getting yourself opened up by the same white coat.”
On the fifth floor, Curtis Nightstar paid his thanks in motion instead of money.
Mika Harima, who had been a package and a lock,
got dipped in a suite that was supposed to be a hiding place —
and for one stupid, bright second,
the reconstruction looked like a girl who had been invited to stay in the room.
Curtis twirled her again.
Then again. The suite carpet took the steps like it had been waiting. Long black braids drew a dark circle in the air. Mika’s borrowed face flashed past the window, the bar, the pink rose, and back.
“We’re just dancing,” he said, easy now that she hadn’t gone rigid. “And I’ve never had a dance partner. Feels like a wasted skill I picked up.” Another turn, careful of her balance. “Plus the way your feet move. Tells me you know how to as well.”
Mika let him take the next spin before she answered. Her body had already voted. Her mouth was late.
“…Middle school festival. Anri stood there like a pole and I had to lead both of us. Then I got pretty and people wanted pictures, not steps.” A breath that was almost a laugh. “Don’t compliment my feet like that’s normal detective work.”
She didn’t pull her hand free.
“Wasted skill, huh. You’re tall. You don’t step on me. That’s already better than most of Ikebukuro.” Another turn, this one hers as much as his. “If the city knocks, I’m blaming the dancing. Until then—”
She looked up at him, reconstruction and all.
“Don’t make it a love story. Just don’t stop yet.”
On the fifth floor, two people the city wanted to file under problem and missing
used a hotel carpet as proof they still had bodies that could choose something gentle.
Curtis Nightstar finally spent the skill.
Mika Harima remembered she had feet that were hers.
Curtis dipped her again.
The city lights ran sideways through the glass. Mika’s hair fell back. His hand stayed sure at her waist, like the suite had rules and one of them was don’t drop the girl who just gave you two doctors.
“Who said anything about a love story?”
Mika smiled at him upside-down.
“Jerk.”
He brought her up laughing under it. She flicked his chest with two fingers, not enough to be a hit.
“You dip a person after they say don’t make it a love story, you don’t get to play innocent. That’s a crime in at least three countries.”
Curtis’s smirk stayed small.
“I’ll turn myself in tomorrow. After the address.”
“After the address,” she agreed, and didn’t let go of his hand right away.
On the fifth floor, it still wasn’t a love story.
It was a missing girl calling a wanderer a jerk
because smiling at him had become easier than being scared —
and Curtis Nightstar, who collected complications like other people collected stamps,
held the dip long enough to let her finish the insult.
After the dance, the suite went ordinary again.
Curtis excused himself to the bathroom. Water on. Shirt off. Long black braids falling forward over a chest that had never learned how to look unwritten. Claw tracks. Blade lines. Punctures. The old whip-maps across muscle that did not belong on a college first-year. He reached for the tap and went still for no good reason, like the mirror had already started the argument.
The door was not fully shut.
Mika’s voice arrived first.
“Hey Curtis, I was just wo—”
Then she was in the frame.
Shirtless. All of it showing. The body the city only got in pieces — hotel bar, blue blazer, a dip on the carpet — suddenly completed in fluorescent light. Green eye. Blue eye. Scars laid over scars until there was almost no unmarked country left.
Curtis went quiet.
Not a joke. Not fuck all kinds of duck. Just the particular silence he used when a shirt came off and another person had to decide if he was still a person.
Mika’s sentence died in her mouth.
She did not scream. She had woken up on a table. She knew what cut skin looked like when it was done on purpose.
“…Oh.”
Her eyes tracked despite herself — shoulder, ribs, the ugly asterisk of something that had gone in and stayed, the long slashes that were not one fight but a life. Then she dragged her gaze back up to his face like looking anywhere else was theft.
“I was going to ask if you wanted the kettle on.” A swallow. “That’s not— Curtis. That’s not a couple of stories. That’s a whole book someone wrote on you without asking.”
She stayed in the doorway. Did not step closer. Did not run.
“You went quiet. I get it. I had a neck they turned into a project. I’m not going to ask you to narrate every line while you’re standing there like that.” A thinner beat. “But don’t hide in the shower like I caught you committing a crime. I already knew you weren’t a normal boy from Raira. This just… explains the pale eyes.”
Curtis still said nothing.
Mika’s voice dropped.
“When you’re done. Shirt on. Come back out. I’ll pretend I came for the kettle if that’s what you need.” She hesitated. “Or I won’t pretend. Your call.”
On the fifth floor, the dance ended in a bathroom doorway.
Mika Harima saw the inventory.
Curtis Nightstar, who could twirl a missing girl and smirk,
went quiet in the one place he could not laugh the scars off —
and the hunt, for a moment, was not Yagiri.
It was whether she would still call him Curtis
now that she had seen the forest on his skin.
